      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The city is a busy railway hub served by both JR and Hankyu lines, with over a thousand restaurants clustered around the stations.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.takatsuki-kankou.org/travel-guide/en/",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Every summer, Takatsuki hosts the Takatsuki Festival and the Takatsuki Jazz Street, events that bring the community together.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://osaka-info.jp/en/spot/tourist-information-takatsuki/",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Imashiro Daio‑no‑Mori Park, built on Yayoi‑period ruins, preserves historic artifacts and is regarded as a national treasure.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.takatsuki-kankou.org/travel-guide/en/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Imashirozuka History Museum displays Kofun‑era haniwa, sarcophagi, and a model diorama that narrates Japan's ancient burial practices.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.takatsuki-kankou.org/travel-guide/en/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Mount Ponpon rises to 678.7 meters, the highest point in Takatsuki, overlooking the city’s low‑lying Yodo River valley.",
